---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import SpectrumTerminal from '../components/about/SpectrumTerminal.astro';
import SkillsGrid from '../components/about/SkillsGrid.astro';
---
<PageLayout
  title="About — Pete Michaliszyn | Polished Racing"
  heading="About Pete"
  subheading="From a ZX Spectrum in Communist Poland to the World Cup paddock."
>
  <!-- Pete Today -->
  <section class="pb-20 md:pb-32">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 md:gap-20 items-start">
        <div>
          <div class="aspect-[3/4] bg-bg-raised rounded-lg overflow-hidden relative mb-6">
            <div class="absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center">
              <div class="text-center">
                <div class="w-16 h-16 mx-auto mb-4 border border-white/10 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center">
                  <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" class="text-text-muted">
                    <path d="M20 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H8a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="12" cy="7" r="4"/>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-text-muted uppercase tracking-widest">Headshot needed</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="absolute inset-0 border border-white/5 rounded-lg"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-accent-red mb-4 block">The Mechanic</span>
          <h2 class="font-display text-display-sm uppercase text-white mb-6">Pete Michaliszyn</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4 text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              I'm a professional bike mechanic with over 14 years at the highest level of the sport — across World Cup downhill, cross-country, and the Olympic Games. From Mojo Suspension to Trek Factory Racing, Atherton Racing, Cannondale Factory Racing, and INEOS Grenadiers, I've built and prepared race-winning machines for World Championship victories, 11 World Cup wins, and two Olympic gold medals.
            </p>
            <p>
              After seven years with Gee Atherton through Trek and Atherton Racing, I joined Cannondale Factory Racing, then moved to INEOS Grenadiers where I was part of the programme that delivered Olympic XCO gold for Tom Pidcock at Tokyo 2021 and Pauline Ferrand-Prévot at Paris 2024. In 2025, I returned to CFR for the current World Cup season.
            </p>
            <p>
              I founded Polished Racing in 2015 as the professional home for everything I do. I was part of the team that brought Atherton Bikes to life — 3D titanium-printed downhill frames. I've worked alongside Red Bull, Trek, Shimano, Fox, SRAM, INEOS Grenadiers, and Cannondale. Every bolt I torque, every bearing I press, every suspension setting I dial — it matters. At the Olympic Games or 60+ mph on a World Cup track, there's no room for "good enough."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <!-- Origin Story -->
  <section class="py-20 md:py-32">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <span class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-accent-red mb-4 block">Origin Story</span>
        <h2 class="font-display text-display-md uppercase text-white mb-8">From Bielawa to the World</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I grew up in Bielawa — a small textile town nestled at the foot of the Owl Mountains in southwestern Poland. Population 32,000. A town built on weaving mills and state-run factories, where chocolate bars were a luxury and Coca-Cola was something you heard about but never tasted.
          </p>
          <p>
            My grandfather Józef worked at <span class="text-white">Diora</span> — Poland's legendary radio factory, just 7 kilometres up the road in Dzierżoniów. At its peak, Diora employed over 6,600 people and produced two-thirds of all radios in Poland. My grandfather lived and breathed electronics — circuits, amplifiers, the invisible architecture of sound.
          </p>
          <p>
            One day, he brought home a <span class="text-white">ZX Spectrum</span>. In Communist Poland, in a small town where almost nobody had a computer, this was extraordinary. The machine cost three months' wages. But Józef understood something about the future that most people didn't.
          </p>
          <p>
            I was five years old. I typed <code class="text-accent-cyan bg-bg-raised px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">10 PRINT "HELLO"</code> and watched the words cascade down the television screen. That moment — the machine doing what I told it — never left me.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ZX Spectrum Terminal -->
  <section class="py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6">
      <SpectrumTerminal />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Journey to Cycling -->
  <section class="py-20 md:py-32">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="max-w-3xl ml-auto">
        <span class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-accent-orange mb-4 block">Chapter Two</span>
        <h2 class="font-display text-display-md uppercase text-white mb-8">The Long Road to Racing</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            After the ZX Spectrum came the Commodore, then the Amiga — I followed the same path as every Polish kid who was lucky enough to have access to computers. Software came on pirated cassette tapes from the computer bazaars. We'd spend weekends at the <span class="text-white">giełda komputerowa</span>, swapping disks and magazines.
          </p>
          <p>
            I moved to the UK and found cycling. Not the road kind — the kind where you point a bike down a mountain at terrifying speed and try to get to the bottom faster than everyone else. Downhill mountain biking grabbed me the way the ZX Spectrum had. There was the same combination of precision engineering and raw thrill.
          </p>
          <p>
            I started taking bikes apart the same way I used to take apart BASIC programs — understanding every component, how they connected, why things worked (and why they broke). Eventually, I was good enough that the professionals noticed. In 2013, I became Gee Atherton's personal mechanic on Trek Factory Racing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest, as they say, is scroll position.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BASIC to Vibe Coding -->
  <section class="py-20 md:py-32 bg-bg-raised/30">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <span class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-accent-cyan mb-4 block">Full Circle</span>
        <h2 class="font-display text-display-md uppercase text-white mb-8">From BASIC to Vibe Coding</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            In 2026, forty years after my grandfather handed me that rubber-keyed machine, I built my first app. Not by writing code — by talking to an AI. They call it <span class="text-white">vibe coding</span>: you describe what you want in plain English and the machine builds it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The language changed from BASIC to English. The interface changed from a rubber keyboard to a conversation. But the principle my grandfather taught me when I was five years old is exactly the same: <span class="text-accent-cyan">a machine does what you tell it</span>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The five-year-old who typed <code class="text-accent-cyan bg-bg-raised px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">PRINT "HELLO FROM BIELAWA"</code> and the forty-something who says "Hey Claude, build me an app" — they're the same person, separated by four decades and a revolution in how humans talk to machines.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <!-- Skills Grid -->
  <section class="py-20 md:py-32">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6">
      <span class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-accent-red mb-4 block">Technical Skills</span>
      <h2 class="font-display text-display-md uppercase text-white mb-12">What I Do</h2>
      <SkillsGrid />
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
